{"ts": 1789702010.798341, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I burned through my entire budget in one batch (I should have been more careful \u2014 I didn't realize the budget of 20 would apply to this large parallel batch and didn't reserve calls to test combinations). Looking at the results I did get:\n\n- Feature 7: Sharpe 0.285 (best single)\n- Feature 17: Sharpe 0.229\n- Feature 5: Sharpe 0.215\n- Feature 3: Sharpe 0.213\n- Feature 16: Sharpe 0.210\n- Feature 6: Sharpe -0.256 (strong negative, i.e., short 6 would give +0.256)\n- Feature 12: Sharpe -0.618 (strong negative, short 12 would give +0.618)\n\nSince I have no more evaluate calls left, I cannot test combinations directly. I'll construct a reasoned combination from the single-feature results: feature 12 (shorted) had by far the strongest signal (-0.618 long \u2192 short gives high positive), combined with feature 7 (long, 0.285) and feature 17 (long, 0.229). These are the three strongest individual absolute-Sharpe signals, so an equal-weight combination (with appropriate signs) is my best bet without further testing. In-sample Sharpes of individual features often shrink out-of-sample and combinations don't add linearly, so I'll set a conservative predicted OOS Sharpe."}
