{"ts": 1789702107.9818518, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted the specification using **feature 7, long-only**, which had the highest in-sample Sharpe (0.285) among all the single-feature evaluations I ran.\n\n**Summary of what happened:**\n- I made an error by firing off 31 parallel `evaluate` calls at once, which exhausted my entire 20-call budget on single-feature tests before I could test any multi-feature combinations.\n- The top signals I found were: feature 7 (+0.285), feature 17 (+0.229), feature 5 (+0.215), feature 3 (+0.213), feature 16 (+0.210), and feature 12 (-0.618, strong short signal).\n- I attempted to submit a 3-feature combination (short 12, long 7, long 17) reasoning it would combine the strongest signals, but the system rejected it because that exact combination was never actually evaluated \u2014 only individually-evaluated specs can be submitted.\n- With zero evaluate calls left, I fell back to submitting the single best **evaluated** specification: feature 7 long-only.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: mean 0.18, sd 0.25** \u2014 shaded down from the in-sample value of 0.285 to account for typical in-sample-to-out-of-sample degradation/overfitting on a single feature, with a wide standard deviation reflecting real uncertainty about generalization.\n\n**Lesson for next time:** batch evaluate calls in smaller groups and reserve a portion of the budget specifically for testing candidate multi-feature/sign combinations before committing to a final submission."}
